<TD WIDTH="4%" VALIGN="top" ALIGN="left"><FONT FACE="Times New Roman" SIZE="2"><B>Q.</B></FONT></TD>
<TD ALIGN="left" VALIGN="top"><FONT FACE="Times New Roman" SIZE="2"><B>How does an investment in an equipment lease compare to an investment in another class of asset, such as real estate? </B></FONT></TD></TR></TABLE>  <P
STYLE="margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:-6px"><FONT SIZE="1">&nbsp;</FONT></P>
<TABLE STYLE="BORDER-COLLAPSE:COLLAPSE" BORDER="0" CELLPADDING="0" CELLSPACING="0" WIDTH="100%">
<TR>
<TD WIDTH="4%" VALIGN="top" ALIGN="left"><FONT FACE="Times New Roman" SIZE="2">A.</FONT></TD>
<TD ALIGN="left" VALIGN="top"><FONT FACE="Times New Roman" SIZE="2">Equipment leases create rental income for their owners as does investment real estate. It is not possible to compare, in general, the likelihood of an equipment lease providing a
satisfactory return to the likelihood of an investment in real estate providing such. In general, the economic life of equipment will be less than the economic life of most real estate investments. As a result, equipment lease rentals include a
return on investment for the leasing company as well as a return of investment representing the portion of equipment life that was used up during the equipment lease term. As a consequence, an investment in an equipment lease can be expected by the
leasing company to have very high rental payments receivable compared to real estate but lower future value of the property. </FONT></TD></TR></TABLE>  <P STYLE="margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px"><FONT SIZE="1">&nbsp;</FONT></P>
<TABLE STYLE="BORDER-COLLAPSE:COLLAPSE" BORDER="0" CELLPADDING="0" CELLSPACING="0" WIDTH="100%">
